text:
As the King, manifesting through the fourfold prosperity, surveyed the world with a gaze of compassion, he saw that due to the karma of sentient beings, they appeared within the lotus flower. Regarding this, he noted that even if three eons were to pass, it would not last, much like seeing a firefly at night; one does not expect it to remain for long. He felt great compassion for sentient beings. He contemplated the culmination of a single day’s intention. He rendered the essence of karmic maturation to be self-liberating.

The mind of kindness is joyful without the four <note>referring to the four immeasurables</note>. 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated, which is virtuous. 
Because the mastery of <term>Om</term> <gloss>the sacred primordial sound</gloss> is not established, it is self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated, which is virtuous. 
Because the darkness of ignorance is self-liberated, 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because the grasping of blessings and winds is not established, it is self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because the six perfections <note>paramitas</note> are not established, they are self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because the owner of the fire <note>referring to internal elemental processes</note> is not established, it is self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because the sound of <term>Hum</term> <gloss>the seed syllable of vajra heart</gloss> is not established, it is self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because the deities are not established, they are self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because meditation is not established, it is self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because emptiness is not established, it is self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated. 
Because the result is not established, it is self-liberated; 
Karma and its maturation are self-liberated, which is virtuous.

Karmic maturation is self-liberating virtue. Karmic maturation is self-liberating. It has become perfect.

<term>Om Mani Padme Hum</term> <gloss>the six-syllabled mantra of Avalokiteshvara</gloss>. Open the gate of the six realms. Sealed by the command.

<summary>This text details the compassionate perspective of the King, who realizes that all phenomena and karmic results are self-liberating, and concludes with the invocation of the six-syllable mantra to benefit sentient beings in the six realms.</summary>
<keywords>Avalokiteshvara, Karma, Self-liberation, Mani Mantra, Compassion, Emptiness</keywords>
